From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Mar 1994 02:52:46 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-176] PCR: Is Certainty Relative?

On Sun, 13 Mar 1994, Ray wrote:

>   I'm not really well-read on philosophy, but didn't Hume argue that
> the only real knowledge is logical/mathematical (i.e. the relationship
> among ideas), that we can have no knowledge of matters of fact (i.e.
> about nature, existence), and therefore science is a fraud, blah blah.

Pretty much true. Hume didn't seriously argue that science was fraud; 
what he was arguing was that no philosopher up to his time had managed to 
explain how it was even possible. And he made a damn good case.

> It is also my impression that Hume is accepted by most modern philosophers.

Yes. But then, most modern philosophers understand little about Popper's 
philosophy of science and have never even heard of Bartley. What's 
exciting about evolutionary epistemology and PCR is that it's the first 
philosophical explanation of the *fact* of human knowledge that actually 
addresses the problem that Hume pointed out, rather than ignoring it and 
hoping it would go away. Kant tried to make a half-assed start, but was 
just wrong about too many things from the get-go.

>   Of course, the only thing this tells me is that philosophy is pretty
> irrelevent when it comes to how people treat the real world, i.e. researchers,
> engineers, and theorists really don't care whether or not someone philosopher
> has shown that there is no logical basis for empiricism/causuality because
> the assumption that there is a basis leads to more useful things than not
> assuming the basis.

Well, philosophy is being metaphorical when it claims to be trying to 
*provide* a basis for empiricism/causality. What it's really trying to do 
is *explain* the basis, so that we can understand how we understand 
things. Sure, human knowledge, scientific knowledge, is a *fact*, but 
explanations of facts are often useful. Evolutionary theory explains the 
existence of living organisms, astronomical theory explains the existence 
of stars and galactic structures. Epistemology seeks to explain human 
knowledge, and how we are even *able* to predict things reliably.

