From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Mon, 14 Mar 94 18:32:43 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-179] LANG: Evolution of language

Simon! Levy originally wrote (X-Message-Number: #94-3-124):
>>>Language clearly evolved at some point in the distant past, but it makes more
>>>sense to say that, since that time, language hasn't evolved, but rather
>>>changed.
>>>The changes are not directed toward some end state; in fact, they are more 
>>>likely cyclic, or like movement through some multi-dimensional state space.

It seemed to me that language evolves, since the general conditions are
satisfied: there are replicators that exert phenotypic effects, and these
replicators have a suitable medium to operate in, together with a means of
propagation.  (See below for further exposition.)  I was mainly thinking
in terms of the more obvious changes to vocabulary, and Simon! did mention
that linguists are more interested in the grammatical and phonetic changes.  
But Simon! wrote that language has now stopped "evolving" and is simply
"changing", and the only reason for this hinted at was that the changes
"are not directed toward some end state".  

I didn't consider this a suitable way to distinguish evolution from change
as I support the view that evolution is about filling niches rather than
"advancement" (back in 1992 I'd been mildly criticised by ddf for
describing insects as "primitive"; he pointed out that they're just as
successful at filling a niche as we are, and he's right).  So I began by
listing some problems with the "evolution is advancement" view:

(From X-Message-Number: #94-3-148)
>>Now if evolution has to be
>>advancement towards some end state, then viruses haven't evolved since
>>Pre-Cambrian times, and insects stopped evolving in the Carboniferous
>>period.  (Although it might not always be possible to be entirely
>>objective about whether a change is an advancement, a stagnation, or a
>>decline.)

I also mentioned that even if you did take this view, you would have to
also expect language not to become any more advanced, for it to be
disqualified as "evolution".  Next I explained that evolution is about
"filling niches":

>>Evolution has been defined as the process by which primordial life has
>>developed by successive stages into the multiplicity of animal and plant
>>life, extinct and existing.  So I'd class any similar process of
>>inaccurate copying as evolution.  One natural consequence of such 
>>evolution is that given sufficient copying and variation, each niche gets
>>filled,

So I was expecting Simon! to either explain the real reason why language
doesn't evolve (most likely), or to defend the "evolution is advancement"
view.  I was surprised to find that he seemed to believe I supported
"evolution is advancement", and then, not surprisingly, argued against it,
but still gave no reason why language doesn't evolve:

(From X-Message-Number: #94-3-172):   >> = sjw   > = s!dl
>> The great thing about biological evolution is that you can start with 
>> primitive, simple objects, and end up with advanced, complex, conscious
>> lifeforms.  It seems as if the changes are directed towards an end state
                  ^^^^^ ^^ ^^
>But this sort of teleolgical view is not accepted in modern biology, for
>a very good reason:  If the changes "are directed", when who is the Director? 
>Furtermore, the idea that evolution entails an increase in complexity has 
>fallen out of favor in biology, probably for similar reasons.  Perhaps you are 
>thinking in terms of the Anthropic Cosmological Principle?
>
>> [...]
>
>How would you measure "advancement"?  Even if you could make such a
>...[arguments in favour of "evolution = filling niches", which I support]
>
>Language doesn't evolve; it just changes.

You don't seem to have given a reason for this; here's some more on why I 
suggest it evolves:

1)  There are replicators that exert phenotypic effects.  Patterns of
neuronal interconnection cause their human hosts to exhibit a particular
behaviour -- i.e. speaking or writing a particular word.  In Richard
Dawkins' *The Selfish Gene* [new 1989 edition], endnotes to chapter 11,
there is mention of a paper by Juan Delius of the University of Konstanz,
Germany, containing a detailed picture of what a meme (the self-
replicating pattern of neuronal wiring) might actually look like.

2)  These replicators have a suitable medium in which to operate.  The
brains of human beings. 

3)  These replicators have a means of propagation.  Take the meme for the
word "Extropian", for instance.  Before the term was coined, there was a
niche waiting to be filled.  The word was conceived, and initially the
meme existed as a structure in one or two brains.  Since then it's
propagated to thousands of other brains, by exerting a particular
behaviour in its hosts (e.g. publishing a mag, setting up an email list,
verbally communicating the word), which is just what genes do to propagate
themselves.

Dawkins mentions clothes fashions and catch-phrases as examples of meme
expression.  Later on, I listed such examples, and suggested that there
isn't much difference between catch-phrases and words:

>> Language requires the same sort
>>of brain to brain propagation as does memetic evolution -- it might be
>>considered a subdivision of memetic evolution, in fact.  A meme or meme-
>>complex could represent a tune, symphony, design of a new product, new
>>scientific theory, or catchphrase, so why not a single word or a vocabulary?

I'm proposing that there is a meme or meme-complex for the whole concept
of extropianism, and the words "Extropian", etc are represented either by
separate memes or by part of the extropianism meme-complex.  Maybe there's
some distinction that I've overlooked, between catch-phrases and
neologisms, or between neologisms and vocabulary learned at an early age,
but as long as neologisms are memes they're part of memetic evolution.  
Then again, it might be that the 'replicating' meme for "triangle" varies
from one person to the next, and it only denotes "triangle" because it's
linked with the memes for "three" and "two-dimensional" which have their
unique interconnections in their turn, etc.  (Though that may just mean
that the structures are more complex but still ultimately equivalent.)

But for memetic evolution to occur independently of genetic evolution / 
posthuman engineering, it clearly has to take place in RAM-type memory, as
I mentioned in the excerpt below.  If other aspects of language such as
syntax are derived from some kind of national firmware, they couldn't be
part of memetic evolution (but then, change would be much harder). 

}}The easiest example of language evolving would be the emergence of
}}neologisms and abandonment of disliked / redundant words.  The structure
}}representing each particular word, its meme, is replicated as it
}}propagates from brain to brain -- a subdivision of memetic evolution.
}}Those words with the higher survival value catch on, and the unpopular
}}ones go out of circulation.  However, vocabulary would be stored in
}}read/write memory.  If other aspects such as grammar and phoneme usage
}}are stored in a nation's collective unconscious / read-only-memory, it
}}seems less sensible to think of this as 'evolving' independently of us.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

