From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Tue, 15 Mar 94 0:04:26 WET
Subject: [#94-3-187] Natural Rights and Ancient Liberties

Stephen J. Whitrow writes:
> From: Tim Starr
> >>because the individual human brain is the only local entity capable of
> >>rational action; by comparison states, economies, ecosystems, etc are
> >>entropic, irrational giants.  Purpose originates from the more complex,
> >>concrete, extropy-generating objects, rather than from abstract, chaotic,
> >>disconnected, postulated wholes.
> >However, I think it's does disservice to social processes to describe them
> >as "entropic" and chaotic.  Rather, I'd say that their order is one that
>  
> Ok, they can be quite orderly; in comparison with the human brain, though,
> they're less extropic.  I'm interested in examining why the methodological
> monists and reductionists are wrong, and I believe this applies in the
> case of sciences of conscious intelligence as well as the social sciences.

    Are you familiar with the concept of spontanaeous order? Have you
ever written an evolutionary algorithm or played with artificial
life and noticed the enormous amount of complexity evolved? It seems
it is this lack of imagination that causes philsophers to misunderstand
the position of reductionists.

  Markets are extremely extropic in comparison with the "order" of command
economies. The next time you buy a piece of candy, think of the
enormous among of large scale organization needed to create it.
The wrapper paper, the writing, the chemicals, the manufacturing,
the transportation, the distribution, etc. Just try to get a single
"extropic" human to produce candy on that scale.

[chart deleted]

> The righthand side compares the methodological monism of eliminative
> materialists / identity theorists / behaviourists vs non-reductionist
> alternatives.  Let's say you want to predict what action a particular
> human would take.  The reductionists would claim that even if you recorded
> full details of the original brain and destroyed it, you'd only need to
> "model" or "simulate" all the particles of the brain on a computer, and
> such an individual would definitely be "conscious", would behave exactly
> as before, etc.  I'm not happy with this methodological monism; it seems
> to me that the individual is more than the sum of hir parts.

   But this is only your feeling, you have no proof. A reductionist
would note that one electron is as good as any other and everyday
millions of atoms in your body are replaced. Thus, there isn't
much evidence that it is the particles themselves that are responsible
for consciousness, but the *organization* and  *transitional* rules
of the system of particles.

> Take the functionalists -- they're materialists, yet do not believe a
> science of conscious intelligence requires a continuity with the natural
> sciences.  Certainly, at the level of economics and social affairs it's
> time to break with the natural sciences; in fact not to do so is
> "scientistic" (according to Hayek).  I suspect the reductionist approach
> applied to conscious intelligence is similarly "scientistic".  Here, the
> correct method would be a top-down approach with the arrows again pointing
> away from the brain, which is where extropy and purpose originate rather
> than from a bunch of particles.

   Order (Extropy, negentropy) isn't only found in the brain however. There
are many other areas of nature which exibit high self-order and in the lab
"bunches of particles" can also be shown to create spontaneous orders.
Freezing water creates lots of order, so does coal-mines+pressure -> diamond.

  Neither top-down nor bottom-up is correct. They must meet in the
middle. For instance, no reductionist in his right mind would try to
model biology from the level of quantum mechanics, or engineer
a pentium processor from the chemist level. Scientists study things
on both the macroscopic level and the microscopic level, in a top-down
approach, and a bottom-up approach. Macroeconomics vs Microeconomics.
At each step of the way, we create new levels of abstraction to more 
easily build upon the foundation we have built and the bottom and the
top influence each other. To claim that reductionism is wrong is 
philosophical nonsense. It overlooks the enormous success of reductionism 
in almost every field of study. 

> But the reductionist approach here is a bottom-up approach -- they would
> claim that by simply "modelling" all the particles there would be no
> subtle changes in behaviour, which seems to me to underestimate the
> extropy generation and importance of the brain.  The statist reductionists
> would claim that the groups of people are more important than the people
> themselves, and that it's more accurate to say that things are caused by 
> the groups, states, etc.  The reductive materialists etc would claim that
> the particles comprising the brain are more important than the brain

   Patently false. First of all, a reductionist would claim that it is
the organization of the particles (the brain) that is important. Secondly, the
correct analogy is particle:individual::brain:society. By claiming that
the brain as a whole is fundamentally important, you are making the same
mistake statists make in elevating the importance of the group above
its constituent parts. Reductionists emphasize the relations between
the individual parts that make up the whole.

> itself.  In both cases the non-reductionist arrow points in a different
> direction in terms of physical size, but in terms of extropy generating
> potential it consistently points from the more extropic level to the less
> extropic, and I suspect this is the key.

   Your chart is nonsense because you fail to define extropy specifically
and to justify your claims such as that economies are less
extropy generating. It's clear to me that if you take an individual 
brain's "extropy" output and compare it with and without an economy,
the economy tends to increase the amount of order by taking individual
extropy and combining it in a fashion that is larger than simply
summing individual contribution. As a small example, this mailing
list probably contains more extropy together than merely measuring
the individuals on this list in isolation. Or consider Extro 1.

